\\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b4a0dd3c5bf7582709a5372b16ca4dee81643fc2d617fc254b70117f32d0a883"},"seconds":39.63189911842346,"seed":1113322538035828355,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for \\(a + b\\) given the equations \\(a^2b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4}\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the second equation in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\) into the first equation \\(a^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} b^3 \\right)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} \\right)^2 (b^3)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{729}{16} \\right) b^9 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(b^9\\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{16}{729}\\):\n\\[\nb^9 = \\frac{32}{27} \\cdot \\frac{16}{729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^9 = \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b^9 = \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^3\\), taking the ninth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(b = \\frac{8}{27}\\) back into the expression for \\(a\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^3\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27 \\cdot 512}{4 \\cdot 19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{13824}{78732}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nFinally, calculate \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{2}{9} + \\frac{8}{27} = \\frac{6}{27} + \\frac{8}{27} = \\frac{14}{27}\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{14}{27}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{14}{27}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for \\(a + b\\) given the equations \\(a^2b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4}\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the second equation in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\) into the first equation \\(a^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} b^3 \\right)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} \\right)^2 (b^3)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{729}{16} \\right) b^9 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(b^9\\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{16}{729}\\):\n\\[\nb^9 = \\frac{32}{27} \\cdot \\frac{16}{729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^9 = \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b^9 = \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^3\\), taking the ninth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(b = \\frac{8}{27}\\) back into the expression for \\(a\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^3\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27 \\cdot 512}{4 \\cdot 19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{13824}{78732}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nFinally, calculate \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{2}{9} + \\frac{8}{27} = \\frac{6}{27} + \\frac{8}{27} = \\frac{14}{27}\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{14}{27}}\n\\]","id":"algebra:912"}
